    {
        "content": "<p>You generally shouldn't quantify over all types, as in <code>∀ {α : Type*} ...</code>, as this is very easy to lead to universe issues like the one you have here. In this case, since formulas can only contain finitely many free variables, I think it should be sufficient to to only quantity over <code>∀ {n : Nat} {φ : L.Formula (Fin n)}, ...</code> and the prove the theorem that this definition implies the version for arbitrary formulas.</p>",
        "id": 578120172,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1773052468
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Universe.20mismatch/near/578120172\">說</a>：</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>only quantity over <code>∀ {n : Nat} {φ : L.Formula (Fin n)}, ...</code> and the prove the theorem that this definition implies the version for arbitrary formulas.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I see. It reminds me of how we do it in <code>ElementaryMaps</code>. Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 578123686,
        "sender_full_name": "Zikang 'Axi' Yu",
        "timestamp": 1773053370
    }
